\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces Canonical weight profile for Kobe Bryant: per metric, the trace-normalized matrix $P^k_p$ of Equation~\ref  {eq:player_profile}. Diagonal cells give the share of the player's curvature energy on each of the metric's canonical components (rows of diagonals sum to 1); off-diagonal cells give the signed interference weights, mean-zero across the population by construction. Bryant is close to a pure canonical ager: the availability and usage metrics (GP\%, MPG, OBPM, USG) concentrate $0.87$--$0.92$ of their energy on their first canonical component with small cross terms, so his aging is well described by the population's dominant shapes at above-average magnitude. Components are metric-specific and not comparable across panels.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.74}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:player_profile_kobe}{{29}{67}{Canonical weight profile for Kobe Bryant: per metric, the trace-normalized matrix $P^k_p$ of Equation~\ref {eq:player_profile}. Diagonal cells give the share of the player's curvature energy on each of the metric's canonical components (rows of diagonals sum to 1); off-diagonal cells give the signed interference weights, mean-zero across the population by construction. Bryant is close to a pure canonical ager: the availability and usage metrics (GP\%, MPG, OBPM, USG) concentrate $0.87$--$0.92$ of their energy on their first canonical component with small cross terms, so his aging is well described by the population's dominant shapes at above-average magnitude. Components are metric-specific and not comparable across panels.\relax }{figure.caption.74}{}}
